    (v_topic_id, 'A 7-year-old child refuses to bear weight on the right leg. The hip is held in flexion, abduction, and external rotation. Temperature is 39.5°C. Laboratory evaluation shows a WBC count of 15,000/mm³ and an ESR of 50 mm/hr. According to the Kocher criteria, what is the probability that this child has septic arthritis rather than transient synovitis?', '["About 50%", "Exactly 0%", "< 10% (Very low probability)", "About 25%", "> 90% (Very high probability)"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The Kocher criteria differentiate septic arthritis from transient synovitis. The 4 criteria are: non-weight-bearing, fever > 38.5°C, ESR > 40 mm/hr, and WBC > 12,000/mm³. Having all 4 predictors yields a 99% probability of septic arthritis; 3 predictors yield a 93% probability. This child has 4 predictors.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'Patient #79888: A 3-year-old child, who is at the 95th percentile for weight, presents with progressively worsening bowing of the left leg (genu varum). Radiographs show a prominent ''beak'' at the medial metaphysis of the proximal tibia. What is the diagnosis?', '["Infantile Blount disease", "Rickets", "Physiologic genu varum", "Achondroplasia", "Osgood-Schlatter disease"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Detailed orthopedic assessment of the pediatric patient is required to identify these specific conditions correctly and avoid serious long-term complications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old female gymnast complains of insidious onset, mechanical low back pain that is exacerbated by extension activities (e.g., back walkovers). Neurologic exam is normal. Oblique radiographs of the lumbar spine demonstrate a ''collar'' on the ''Scotty dog''. What is the specific anatomic defect in this condition?', '["Fracture of the transverse process", "Congenital failure of segmentation of the lumbar vertebrae", "Herniation of the nucleus pulposus", "Defect in the pars interarticularis", "Defect in the vertebral body endplate (Schmorl node)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Spondylolysis is a stress fracture or defect of the pars interarticularis. It is highly prevalent in adolescent athletes participating in sports involving repetitive lumbar hyperextension. On oblique radiographs, a radiolucent gap in the pars resembles a collar on the neck of the ''''Scotty dog''''.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 9-year-old basketball player presents with posterior heel pain. The pain worsens after running and improves with rest. Examination reveals tenderness upon medial-lateral compression of the calcaneal tuberosity (positive ''squeeze test''). There is tightness of the Achilles tendon. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Calcaneal apophysitis (Sever disease)", "Tarsal coalition", "Achilles tendon rupture", "Plantar fasciitis", "Calcaneal stress fracture"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sever disease is a traction apophysitis of the calcaneus where the Achilles tendon inserts. It is a common cause of heel pain in athletic children aged 8-12. The classic exam finding is pain with medial/lateral compression of the heel. Treatment includes heel cups, calf stretching, and NSAIDs.'),
