    (v_topic_id, 'A 14-year-old soccer player complains of anterior knee pain that is worse after practice and jumping. Examination reveals point tenderness and a bony prominence over the tibial tubercle. Radiographs show fragmentation of the tibial tubercle apophysis. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Surgical excision of the ossicle", "Immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 weeks", "Strict non-weight bearing with crutches", "Intra-articular corticosteroid injection", "Activity modification, ice, NSAIDs, and reassurance"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle, common in active adolescents experiencing rapid growth. It is a self-limiting condition. Treatment is symptomatic with rest, ice, NSAIDs, and physical therapy (quadriceps/hamstring stretching).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 15-year-old soccer player complains of anterior knee pain that is worse after practice and jumping. Examination reveals point tenderness and a bony prominence over the tibial tubercle. Radiographs show fragmentation of the tibial tubercle apophysis. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 weeks", "Strict non-weight bearing with crutches", "Intra-articular corticosteroid injection", "Activity modification, ice, NSAIDs, and reassurance", "Surgical excision of the ossicle"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle, common in active adolescents experiencing rapid growth. It is a self-limiting condition. Treatment is symptomatic with rest, ice, NSAIDs, and physical therapy (quadriceps/hamstring stretching).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old basketball player complains of anterior knee pain that is worse after practice and jumping. Examination reveals point tenderness and a bony prominence over the tibial tubercle. Radiographs show fragmentation of the tibial tubercle apophysis. What is the most appropriate management?', '["Intra-articular corticosteroid injection", "Activity modification, ice, NSAIDs, and reassurance", "Strict non-weight bearing with crutches", "Surgical excision of the ossicle", "Immobilization in a long leg cast for 6 weeks"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osgood-Schlatter disease is a traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle, common in active adolescents experiencing rapid growth. It is a self-limiting condition. Treatment is symptomatic with rest, ice, NSAIDs, and physical therapy (quadriceps/hamstring stretching).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 2-week-old infant, born breech, presents for a routine well-child check. Examination reveals asymmetric thigh skin folds and a positive Ortolani maneuver on the right. What is the most appropriate initial imaging modality to confirm the diagnosis?', '["AP radiograph of the pelvis", "Ultrasound of the hips", "CT scan of the pelvis", "MRI of the hips", "Bone scan"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Developmental Dysplasia of the Hip (DDH) is most accurately assessed with ultrasound in infants under 4-6 months of age because the femoral head and acetabulum are predominantly cartilaginous and not fully visible on radiographs. A positive Ortolani test (reduction of a dislocated hip) strongly suggests DDH.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 24-day-old infant is brought to the clinic for unilateral right clubfoot (talipes equinovarus). The parents ask about the treatment plan. According to the Ponseti method, the deformities are corrected with serial casting in a specific sequence. Which component of the deformity is corrected LAST?', '["Cavus", "Adductus", "Varus", "Equinus", "Supination"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In the Ponseti method for clubfoot, the CAVE deformities are corrected sequentially: Cavus (elevating the first ray), Adductus, Varus, and finally Equinus. The equinus correction often requires a percutaneous Achilles tenotomy.');
